    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i've also heard people say that originalism is just a way for judges to push their own political agendas through a legal lens. how do scholars usually respond to that critique?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Skeptics argue that \"original intent\" is often too ambiguous for judges to definitively establish, leading to subjective interpretations that favor a political agenda. In response, strict originalists often focus on the \"public meaning\" of the words at the time, arguing this more objective approach is less political. Others argue that *all* judicial interpretation is inherently political, but originalism at least provides a principled check against judicial activism.",
      "source": "target",
